WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:16.639
Hey there, welcome to the POSIT Data Science Hangout. I'm Libby Herron, and this is a recording

00:00:16.639 --> 00:00:22.000
of our weekly community call that happens every Thursday at 12 p.m. U.S. Eastern Time.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:27.240
If you are not joining us live, you miss out on the amazing chat that's going on. So find

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:30.680
the link in the description where you can add our call to your calendar and come hang

00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:36.320
out with the most supportive, friendly, and funny data community you'll ever experience.

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:41.119
Can't wait to see you there. Okay, everybody, let's go ahead and get started. I'm so excited

00:00:41.119 --> 00:00:48.939
to introduce our featured leader today. She is a Data Science Hangout crew member. I met

00:00:48.939 --> 00:00:53.560
her through the Data Science Hangout years ago now, which is wild to think about. It's

00:00:53.560 --> 00:00:59.720
Liz Eisenhauer, Senior Statistical Associate at Westat. Liz, it's so great to have you

00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:03.919
here. Could you give us a little introduction? Tell us a little bit about what you do and

00:01:03.919 --> 00:01:05.720
something you like to do for fun.

00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:10.599
Absolutely. It's really, really amazing to be here. This is such a great community. I'm

00:01:10.599 --> 00:01:15.480
grateful to have been able to join whenever I can over the past few years. And it's nothing

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:22.080
without its organizers. So you guys are really incredible. I will start and say that any

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:27.040
of my views are my own and they're not the views of my organizations or affiliations

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:36.440
or any of that. My kind of identification is as a statistician. So I'd like to start

00:01:36.440 --> 00:01:42.800
by saying what that means to me because that kind of has defined my entire career. If you've

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:50.519
ever done a t-test or any, you know, thought about statistics at all, statistical thinking,

00:01:50.519 --> 00:01:54.639
then I would consider you a statistician. So I just want to say that up front when I

00:01:54.639 --> 00:02:03.080
say statistician, I'm talking about you. This kind of my background is that when I was in

00:02:03.080 --> 00:02:08.279
high school, I took a statistics class at my local community college down in South Jersey.

00:02:08.279 --> 00:02:15.080
So down near the shore in Seattle City, I waitressed in Seattle City for many years

00:02:15.080 --> 00:02:20.479
when I was younger. So I took this community college statistics course and it was, you know,

00:02:20.479 --> 00:02:25.000
great. I was like, this is really cool. And I think I'm decently good at it. So I should keep

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:32.080
going with this. But I kept kind of going with statistics. And every time I would finish a goal,

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:38.440
I would be like, am I a statistician yet? Like, like I finished the course and I was like, okay,

00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:43.880
I think I can go do statistics for, you know, my bachelor's degree. And I went and I got my

00:02:43.960 --> 00:02:47.800
bachelor's degree in math with a statistics specialization, but I was like, I still don't

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:54.279
really know what it means to be a statistician. So I went and did the PhD and I got a PhD in

00:02:54.279 --> 00:02:59.479
statistics. And then I was thinking about going and teaching statistics, like in a kind of academic

00:03:00.039 --> 00:03:05.960
space. And I was like, well, I don't know if I feel like I can tell students that I'm a statistician,

00:03:05.960 --> 00:03:13.240
like have I really been a statistician yet? So I went to this company that I'm currently

00:03:13.960 --> 00:03:20.360
located in and it's called Westat. So this is, I don't know if this is true, but it seems to me

00:03:20.360 --> 00:03:25.639
like it's one of the biggest companies that's completely founded by statisticians. So these

00:03:25.639 --> 00:03:33.800
were, this was a group of statisticians that lived out in Colorado and they founded this company to

00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:40.839
do work for the federal government mostly and large surveys. And they called it Westat because

00:03:40.839 --> 00:03:48.679
they were in the West and they were statisticians. And so they called it Westat. And then of course,

00:03:48.679 --> 00:03:52.039
they realized that if they wanted to do work for the federal government, they would need to move

00:03:52.039 --> 00:03:56.919
to the East coast to be closer to DC. So they eventually moved to the East coast, which made

00:03:56.919 --> 00:04:03.960
the name kind of didn't really make sense anymore. But this pretty large company founded by

00:04:03.960 --> 00:04:11.160
statisticians. And so now I get to be a statistician and I feel like I've, you know, finally

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:15.960
made it. But at this point I feel like I was a statistician back when I did my first t-test.

00:04:17.559 --> 00:04:24.519
So that's my kind of conclusion about how I got to where I am today. And I guess the other

00:04:24.519 --> 00:04:30.200
questions were where, I mean, I live in New Jersey now. I've started in New Jersey. I went Penn State.

00:04:30.200 --> 00:04:34.920
I went Boston for a short time. My husband's from Boston area. I wish I was still there so

00:04:34.920 --> 00:04:41.480
I could join the hangout, you know, gatherings that you all have there. But now I'm in North

00:04:41.480 --> 00:04:50.119
Jersey. My husband got a job at Merck. So we're hanging out in Rahway. And so what I do for fun,

00:04:50.119 --> 00:04:56.279
I think I spend a lot of time thinking about statistics. I don't know how to do other things.

00:04:56.279 --> 00:05:01.160
I do listen to audiobooks and I guess I listened to a pretty good audiobook recently

00:05:01.720 --> 00:05:08.119
called The Correspondent, which was, it's a fiction book, which I sometimes, I try to listen

00:05:08.119 --> 00:05:12.920
to fiction every once in a while. I do listen to a lot of non-fiction, but The Correspondent

00:05:12.920 --> 00:05:19.320
has been pretty interesting. It's a series of letters. The Correspondent by Virginia Evans,

00:05:19.320 --> 00:05:24.760
maybe? I think so. Yeah, that sounds right. I'm going to put a link in the chat.

00:05:25.959 --> 00:05:34.040
Okay. For anybody curious, I also put a poll in the Slido. If you were looking at the Slido

00:05:34.040 --> 00:05:37.640
in your browser, there's a little slider at the top between Q&A and polls.

00:05:39.959 --> 00:05:45.239
And so far we have 11 votes that my question was, have you ever felt statistics imposter syndrome,

00:05:45.239 --> 00:05:50.040
which is unnecessary feelings of inadequacy. I don't really like the phrase imposter syndrome.

00:05:50.040 --> 00:05:55.640
I like unnecessary feelings of inadequacy a little bit better. But so far, not one person

00:05:55.640 --> 00:06:00.920
has said no. Everybody has said yes. I think this might be a universal statsy feeling.

00:06:03.559 --> 00:06:08.839
But I also identify with taking a stats course and being like, oh, I like this. I don't want

00:06:08.839 --> 00:06:17.959
to do anything else. I really love this thing. But then I could not find anyone who needed me

00:06:17.959 --> 00:06:22.920
to do statistics. I got out of school and absolutely nobody needed me to do stats.

00:06:24.040 --> 00:06:29.559
And I realized that that was going to be a really hard thing to do. And when I met you, I was like,

00:06:29.559 --> 00:06:35.239
what is Westat? There's a whole organization of consultants who are doing statistics.

00:06:35.239 --> 00:06:38.920
This is amazing. I have since learned that there are lots of people doing statistics in

00:06:38.920 --> 00:06:44.679
lots of little places, including program evaluation, which was a whole new world to

00:06:44.679 --> 00:06:52.200
me. I know that you have some program evaluation on your resume as well. So I was hoping that you

00:06:52.200 --> 00:06:56.920
could talk a little bit about the types of work that you do, the types of problems that you get

00:06:56.920 --> 00:07:02.679
to solve, the types of data that you get to work with, and also the types of tools that you like

00:07:02.679 --> 00:07:09.640
to use. So like, did you learn everything in Excel and on paper? Were you an SPSS? Are you

00:07:09.640 --> 00:07:18.200
an R person? Are you a Python person? All that stuff. Sure. Yeah. Yeah. I think R has been kind

00:07:18.200 --> 00:07:28.119
of my main language since I was in the bachelor's degree program. R is just a great, yeah, I use it

00:07:28.119 --> 00:07:33.239
for everything, except when I have to use something else, then I use something else.

00:07:33.239 --> 00:07:40.839
But the thing that I'm kind of excited about in my work, and that has come up a lot in program

00:07:40.839 --> 00:07:46.760
evaluation context, like I said, Westat started as a survey organization. So they're very

00:07:47.480 --> 00:07:57.239
interested in really rigorous probability surveys. And they also have work that looks at

00:07:57.239 --> 00:08:04.679
if you have a program, can we determine whether this program works for whom and provide additional

00:08:04.679 --> 00:08:12.359
information to improve the program or policy? Policy is not similar questions. And so that work

00:08:12.359 --> 00:08:20.119
has kind of branched my statistical expertise into these two areas. And one area that's common

00:08:20.119 --> 00:08:25.559
across both of them that I've found to be really interesting recently is sample size justification.

00:08:26.279 --> 00:08:32.840
So when you start a study, I'm really interested in how people who are statsy or statisticians

00:08:32.840 --> 00:08:40.679
can join at the beginning of a project or beginning of a research endeavor and provide

00:08:40.679 --> 00:08:48.440
statistical justification for why this is worth it. I think a lot of science rests on

00:08:49.400 --> 00:08:52.919
statistical shoulders in terms of us being able to say,

00:08:55.000 --> 00:08:59.799
we should do this, and this is why we should do this. We should spend this money, we should

00:08:59.799 --> 00:09:06.520
collect this data, we should put together this infrastructure, because this is my statistical

00:09:06.520 --> 00:09:14.760
argument. And whether that's in a large survey context, where you often want a representative

00:09:14.760 --> 00:09:23.000
estimate for the US population a lot of times, or a large population, and you're interested in a

00:09:23.000 --> 00:09:31.799
really rigorous mean and confidence interval for a particular value, then you might look at the

00:09:31.799 --> 00:09:38.679
precision of that estimate and argue for why you need the certain number of people that you need,

00:09:38.679 --> 00:09:43.719
and you need it to be designed in a particular way so that you can get this precision in your

00:09:43.719 --> 00:09:49.080
estimate. Versus in something like a program evaluation, where you want to estimate the effect

00:09:49.080 --> 00:09:55.799
of something, your argument is we need to randomize, we need to put together this particular

00:09:55.799 --> 00:10:05.479
study design, and the goal is to have a particular statistical power for the effect that we're trying

00:10:05.479 --> 00:10:09.640
to estimate. So there are different kinds of studies, different kinds of ways of making the

00:10:09.640 --> 00:10:16.679
statistical argument at the beginning of a study, and I'm really interested in what that all means.

00:10:16.679 --> 00:10:22.359
I know there's a lot going on right now with funding mechanisms, and unless you've been living

00:10:22.359 --> 00:10:27.080
under a rock, you've probably heard about some grant things that have been happening. And a lot of this

00:10:27.080 --> 00:10:34.520
happens at the stage of designing studies, and so the statistical arguments behind these studies are

00:10:34.520 --> 00:10:40.919
just really fascinating to me. There seem to be two kind of camps of statisticians. There's the

00:10:40.919 --> 00:10:46.520
one that's like doing the calculation, that's like, you know, do whatever you can afford.

00:10:47.559 --> 00:10:53.880
If you can afford it, have a giant study. And then there's the camp of statisticians who are saying,

00:10:54.440 --> 00:11:01.080
we're reviewing these, and you need to give me a solid argument. So if you are telling me

00:11:01.080 --> 00:11:07.559
the effect size is 0.2, give me evidence that you actually think the effect size is 0.2.

00:11:07.559 --> 00:11:14.760
Show me previous data, show me pilot studies, show me something that convinces me that this is what

00:11:14.760 --> 00:11:19.000
you're actually going to see in the study. So there's two camps of statisticians on this, and I

00:11:19.000 --> 00:11:25.479
think it's a really open area that I'm working in this area right now. A lot of my work

00:11:25.479 --> 00:11:32.039
ends up falling in these series of questions. So that's kind of the big picture.

00:11:33.159 --> 00:11:36.119
We're going to continue talking about that, because we have some questions about this in

00:11:36.119 --> 00:11:45.159
the Slido. And I love Rowan's question, which has bubbled to the top here. It has an asterisk,

00:11:45.159 --> 00:11:50.919
so I will ask it for Rowan. And I know that you and I have talked about this, so let's dive in.

00:11:50.919 --> 00:11:56.440
How do you grapple with conveying statistical findings to audiences who are not well-versed

00:11:56.440 --> 00:12:01.640
in statistics? I have found statistics to be particularly difficult to properly explain

00:12:01.640 --> 00:12:09.080
at a layman level without losing nuance. Yeah, nuance is a big part of it, right?

00:12:10.520 --> 00:12:16.520
The statistical nuance that statisticians always end the conversation with like a caveat, right?

00:12:17.239 --> 00:12:21.640
But we can't really say this with certainty. I was saying in the chat when you were like,

00:12:21.640 --> 00:12:29.400
am I a statistician yet? I said, it depends. If you can answer everything with it depends,

00:12:29.400 --> 00:12:36.359
you might have made it to the statistics brain level. Because I feel like when I was learning,

00:12:36.359 --> 00:12:39.880
I was really frustrated by it depends. I was mad about it. I'm like, give me an answer, right?

00:12:39.880 --> 00:12:43.799
Like this is so frustrating that everything is it depends. You're going to make me use critical

00:12:43.799 --> 00:12:49.159
thinking skills for every single question? Come on. But then at some point, people were asking me

00:12:49.159 --> 00:12:54.599
questions. And I was like, oh, it does depend. It depends on so many things. I can't give you

00:12:54.599 --> 00:12:59.239
an answer either. The teacher had become the master, yeah. Or the student had become the

00:12:59.239 --> 00:13:04.919
teacher. But you and I have talked about boundary objects, which is one thing that could be helpful.

00:13:04.919 --> 00:13:09.239
So take it away, Liz. Yeah, yeah, yeah. I found it to be...

00:13:09.239 --> 00:13:15.719
So I work a lot with other technical people, people who are very technically minded,

00:13:15.719 --> 00:13:21.719
but not necessarily statisticians or they don't necessarily have a background in statistics.

00:13:21.719 --> 00:13:27.960
And so that perspective and that kind of interaction is often more interesting to me

00:13:27.960 --> 00:13:37.159
than talking to a lay person or a person that really has no skin... I don't know.

00:13:37.960 --> 00:13:43.320
They're not like stakeholders in whatever terminology you want to use. They're not

00:13:43.320 --> 00:13:49.960
actually one of the people that's making the decisions. Obviously, this is a whole other area

00:13:49.960 --> 00:13:57.159
of interest, people who are thinking about all the different types of people that

00:13:57.159 --> 00:14:01.799
could be involved in decision making. But in my experience, a lot of times when I'm talking to

00:14:01.799 --> 00:14:08.359
technical people, the question is really, can I find something that they understand?

00:14:09.640 --> 00:14:17.960
And can I then latch onto that and connect it to something that I'm trying to say?

00:14:18.679 --> 00:14:25.400
And that connection often happens more easily for me when I have something separate from what I'm

00:14:25.400 --> 00:14:32.440
saying, like a visual of some sort. And so I found flowcharts. I make flowcharts

00:14:33.320 --> 00:14:38.359
for all my projects. Anytime there's a question and someone is having a technical conversation

00:14:38.359 --> 00:14:42.200
and they're trying to figure out, well, this affects this and that affects that. How does

00:14:42.200 --> 00:14:47.479
this all work together? I make a flowchart and I send it to everyone and I say, this is probably

00:14:47.479 --> 00:14:53.000
wrong. Tell me where it's wrong. Let's fix it. Let's make this something that everyone can agree

00:14:53.000 --> 00:14:59.320
on. And then once you have the one term for this kind of thing is boundary objects,

00:14:59.880 --> 00:15:06.599
Susan Lee Starr is a person who came up with this terminology for objects that have

00:15:07.400 --> 00:15:13.159
meaning to different groups, but they mean something different to the two different groups.

00:15:13.799 --> 00:15:18.840
So you can use them to collaborate or coordinate around something because

00:15:19.159 --> 00:15:25.719
they have meaning for both groups. But the statisticians, I see it as, how do we adjust

00:15:25.719 --> 00:15:31.239
for these different things? How do we think about the causal relationships? Whereas maybe

00:15:31.799 --> 00:15:38.039
I put numbers on it, like this is the total number of people in this particular node on my flowchart.

00:15:38.919 --> 00:15:43.479
Someone who comes from a different technical background might say, okay, I understand that

00:15:43.479 --> 00:15:47.080
there are a certain number of people in this node and that's what that means.

00:15:48.039 --> 00:15:51.799
So they understand it a little bit differently, but we can have a conversation

00:15:52.440 --> 00:15:58.119
looking at the same visual. So that's something that I have found to be really useful. In general,

00:15:58.119 --> 00:16:01.479
I mean, just connecting with what people know, meeting people where they are is

00:16:02.520 --> 00:16:08.119
probably pretty good practice. Meet people where they are, figure out what their understanding is

00:16:08.119 --> 00:16:15.239
right now. This is important in all kinds of teaching. And also with toddlers, you look at

00:16:15.239 --> 00:16:20.280
them and you're like, what are you doing? Your adult brain says, this kid's making a mess. This

00:16:20.280 --> 00:16:25.320
kid's destroying something. But then if you ask yourself what they're seeing, they're experiencing

00:16:25.320 --> 00:16:29.960
something, right? That's all their own. They're like sensory experience or visual experience or

00:16:29.960 --> 00:16:34.200
whatever it is. It's totally different from whatever your adult perspective is of what

00:16:34.200 --> 00:16:41.640
that toddler is doing. And I feel like that is so similar to being a stats person or being a

00:16:41.640 --> 00:16:45.960
technical person and talking to someone that you are assuming is non-technical. They're a fully

00:16:45.960 --> 00:16:50.840
fledged human being, an adult. They're so smart and capable. They're just seeing something in a

00:16:50.840 --> 00:16:56.840
different way from you. Figure out what that is so that you can build bridges of connection between

00:16:56.840 --> 00:17:03.320
what they are thinking and what you need them to know about this. Okay. Teacher, teacher soapbox

00:17:03.320 --> 00:17:07.560
off the teacher soapbox Libby. Okay. Let's go back to Slido. Let's find some more questions.

00:17:08.520 --> 00:17:13.479
We had another one from Jared Allen. Jared has an asterisk. I'm going to ask for them.

00:17:14.439 --> 00:17:19.880
What is the most counterintuitive result that a stats analysis has thrown at you?

00:17:19.880 --> 00:17:25.000
Can you think of anything? Share in the chat too, if anybody has any that, that,

00:17:25.000 --> 00:17:33.239
that they're thinking of counterintuitive result. I mean, a lot of something that's interesting

00:17:33.400 --> 00:17:41.640
because I work in a policy space. Sometimes things that you think are going to change,

00:17:41.640 --> 00:17:50.040
don't change, right? Like particular types of measures. I did a study in my PhD where we were

00:17:50.040 --> 00:17:58.599
looking at students attitudes towards probability and the SATS, the survey of attitudes towards

00:17:58.599 --> 00:18:05.000
statistics was the instrument that I use to, to try to look at probability, not statistics.

00:18:05.800 --> 00:18:12.199
And it barely changes. Like the average, you know, students take a probability class and it goes a

00:18:12.199 --> 00:18:20.040
little bit up. Their attitudes barely changed on average. And I found since then that there are some

00:18:20.839 --> 00:18:28.680
scenarios where you just don't see the sensitivity to change that you would expect because either the

00:18:28.680 --> 00:18:38.439
measurement mechanism doesn't change or because the intervention itself is just in such a complex

00:18:38.439 --> 00:18:46.040
environment. Another case that I've seen is in job trainings. Like I've, I've gone to APAM,

00:18:46.439 --> 00:18:51.959
which is the association for public policy analysis and management conference. They had a

00:18:51.959 --> 00:18:57.880
session on job trainings and they showed that in almost all the cases, none of them had any

00:18:57.880 --> 00:19:05.239
significant differences. It was just a scenario where it's really hard to show change. So that

00:19:05.239 --> 00:19:11.880
can be kind of frustrating and also interesting. I love this. I will say in the chat, we are saying

00:19:11.880 --> 00:19:15.880
Simpson's paradox, right? Like that's, that's the funnest one, right? Where you've got,

00:19:15.880 --> 00:19:22.119
if you could imagine like a scatterplot where you have this big unified group of like

00:19:22.839 --> 00:19:27.000
positive association, and then you turn all the groups, different colors, and you realize that

00:19:27.000 --> 00:19:32.760
it's actually just negative associations that are stacked up together that look like one big

00:19:32.760 --> 00:19:39.400
positive association or vice versa. But another one that I really like is in marketing as a

00:19:39.400 --> 00:19:47.640
discipline. When you ask people what they want, they're often just wrong according to their

00:19:47.640 --> 00:19:54.199
actual behavior, right? They'll be like, what do I want? I want A or I want C or I want B, whatever.

00:19:54.199 --> 00:19:58.280
And they, people don't actually know what they want ahead of time. And then when you study their

00:19:58.280 --> 00:20:03.640
behavior, what they actually choose when they're in a store or retail environment or online,

00:20:03.640 --> 00:20:09.319
whatever is incorrect. That's, that's not right. Or study UX design is another thing. Like someone

00:20:09.319 --> 00:20:13.160
might say, oh, I would want a button that does this. And then in reality, when you test their

00:20:13.160 --> 00:20:17.160
user behavior, they don't click that button. They didn't want that button. They want something else,

00:20:17.160 --> 00:20:24.359
but they don't know. I really love it. Okay. Fantastic question, Jared. Let's head to Slido

00:20:24.359 --> 00:20:30.199
and ask Natasha's question. Another start. Nobody wants to ask out loud today. I'll read

00:20:31.000 --> 00:20:33.319
to be everyone asking the question. So I get this.

00:20:36.359 --> 00:20:42.520
What does a typical day or week look like for you? What areas do you split your time between

00:20:42.520 --> 00:20:47.079
programming meetings, for example? Yeah, that's a great question.

00:20:47.880 --> 00:20:52.839
There is a lot of both of those things, programming and meetings. And

00:20:53.800 --> 00:21:05.319
I work fully remote, as a lot of statisticians do. So I am looking at what the tasks of the day are.

00:21:06.760 --> 00:21:12.760
What are my deadlines that I have to make? If I have meetings where I'm advising,

00:21:12.760 --> 00:21:17.640
sometimes I have some of these projects where I'm in an advisory role, kind of

00:21:18.199 --> 00:21:24.359
coming in like on a power analysis and coming in with kind of suggestions or questions,

00:21:24.359 --> 00:21:28.520
and I need to make sure that I'm prepared to come into the meeting and have those conversations.

00:21:30.040 --> 00:21:35.239
And then other projects where there's an analysis that has to get done, and I'm

00:21:36.280 --> 00:21:42.599
walking off time to do that. So yeah, it really depends on the week.

